<%#
  The status Turbo Frame. Two server-side write paths target this same frame id,
  so either swaps in cleanly:
    1. GenerationRequest#broadcast_status_to_self replaces it live after
       succeeded! / failed! — the instant happy-path update.
    2. EndpointsController#status re-renders it for the status-reconciler
       Stimulus controller, which reloads the frame on an interval until terminal
       — closing the Action Cable subscribe race when a run finishes before the
       browser subscribes (e.g. an instant "model not loaded" failure).

  Both write paths drive the frame programmatically (broadcast replace / src
  reload), so target: "_top" does not affect them — it only governs navigations
  initiated by the frame's own links. The terminal panels carry links the
  operator clicks ("Try again" → the form, "View all live endpoints", the live
  endpoint URL), each a full page with no matching frame. Without _top, Turbo
  would scope those clicks to this frame, find no matching frame in the response,
  and silently do nothing. _top lets them navigate the whole page as intended.
%>
<%= turbo_frame_tag dom_id(generation_request), target: "_top" do %>
  <div id="<%= dom_id(generation_request) %>-status">
    <%= render "status", generation_request: generation_request %>
  </div>
<% end %>
